The lone '''Legislative District of Maguindanao del Sur''' is the representation of the [[Provinces of the Philippines|province]] of [[Maguindanao del Sur]] in the [[Philippine House of Representatives]]. [[Maguindanao del Sur]] was part of the representation of [[Legislative districts of Maguindanao|Maguindanao]] prior to its division in [[2022 Maguindanao division plebiscite|2022]]. It will be granted its representation in the [[19th Congress of the Philippines|19th Congress]].

== History==
{{further|Legislative district of Mindanao and Sulu|Legislative district of Cotabato|Legislative districts of Maguindanao}}

Before gaining separate representation, areas now under the jurisdiction of Maguindanao del Sur were represented under the [[Department of Mindanao and Sulu]] (1917–1935), [[Cotabato]] (1935–1972), and [[Maguindanao]] (1984–2007, 2010–2022).

The enactment of Presidential Decree No. 341 on November 22, 1973, created the province of [[Maguindanao]] out of [[Cotabato]]'s [[Maguindanao people|Maguindanao-majority]] municipalities.<ref name=pd341>{{cite web|title=Presidential Decree No. 341 - Creating the Provinces of North Cotabato, Maguindanao and Sultan Kudarat |url=http://www.officialgazette.gov.ph/1973/11/22/presidential-decree-no-341-s-1973/ |website=Official Gazette of the Republic of the Philippines |last=Marcos |first=Ferdinand E. |access-date=November 19, 2017 |date=November 22, 1973}}</ref> The new province was represented in the [[Interim Batasang Pambansa]] as part of [[Interim Batasang Pambansa#Regional Representatives|Region XII]] from 1978 to 1984.

Maguindanao del Sur first gained separate representation in [[1984 Philippine parliamentary election|1984]], when it returned [[#At-Large (defunct)|two representatives, elected at large]], to the [[Regular Batasang Pambansa]].

Under the new Constitution which was proclaimed on February 11, 1987, Maguindanao del Sur, was retained into one congressional district;<ref>{{cite web|title=1987 Constitution of the Philippines - Apportionment Ordinance|website=Official Gazette of the Republic of the Philippines|url=http://www.officialgazette.gov.ph/constitutions/the-1987-constitution-of-the-republic-of-the-philippines/the-1987-constitution-of-the-republic-of-the-philippines-ordinance/|author=1986 Constitutional Commission|date=February 2, 1987|access-date=November 19, 2017}}</ref> each elected its member to the restored [[House of Representatives of the Philippines|House of Representatives]] starting [[1987 Philippine legislative election|that same year]].

The enactment of Republic Act No. 11550 on May 28, 2021, separated the former province of Maguindanao into the provinces of Maguindanao del Norte and Maguindanao del Sur<ref>{{cite web|title=Republic Act No. 11550 - Charter of Maguindanao del Norte and Maguindanao del Sur|url=https://www.officialgazette.gov.ph/downloads/2021/05may/20210527-RA-11550-RRD.pdf|author=Congress of the Philippines|date=May 28, 2021|access-date=May 28, 2021}}</ref> and its subsequent [[2022 Maguindanao division plebiscite|ratification by plebiscite]] on September 17, 2022, separated from Maguindanao's [[Legislative district of Maguindanao#2nd District (defunct)|second district]] 25 municipalities to create the province of [[Maguindanao del Sur]]. Per Section 9 of R.A. 11550, these new provinces were to comprise [[#Lone District|a single congressional district]]; voters began to elect the new province's separate representative beginning in [[2025 Philippine House of Representatives elections|2025]].

